nd"},{"surface":"அணிய","parts":[{"text":"அணி","lemma":"அணி","gloss":"wear"},{"text":"அ","lemma":"அ","gloss":"infinitive suffix"}],"gloss":"to wear"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"வாடாத்","parts":[{"text":"வாடு","lemma":"வாடு","gloss":"fade"},{"text":"ஆ","lemma":"ஆ","gloss":"negative relative participle suffix"},{"text":"த்","lemma":"த்","gloss":"sandhi"}],"gloss":"unfading"},{"surface":"தாமரை","parts":[{"text":"தாமரை","lemma":"தாமரை","gloss":"lotus"}],"gloss":"lotus"},{"surface":"சூட்டுவன்","parts":[{"text":"சூட்டு","lemma":"சூட்டு","gloss":"adorn"},{"text":"வ்","lemma":"வ்","gloss":"future tense infix"},{"text":"அன்","lemma":"அன்","gloss":"third person singular masculine suffix"}],"gloss":"he will crown"},{"surface":"நினக்கே","parts":[{"text":"நினக்கே","lemma":"நினக்கே","gloss":"to you indeed"}],"gloss":"to you indeed","split_unverified":true}]}],"literal":"Dug from the well in the red-soil hollow / muddy little water poured small house / in the courtyard was the old-mouthed jar / we drank the toddy and it is empty and spotless / in the foliage courtyard, the small millet drying / for the doves and quails to eat completely / the time for putting it out has become night, therefore / even if it is roasted hare, we will give, enter / and stay here, O wise-mouthed bard! / The curved-horned wild cow's trembling-headed calf / sparse-haired children harness as a calf / the small village's chief, yesterday / went with the duty sent by the king, having returned, your / songstress to wear a garland / an unfading lotus he will crown you indeed.","translation":"O wise bard! Stay here with us. The old jar in the courtyard of our small house, which held the muddy water dug from the well in the red-soil hollow, is now empty and clean because we drank all the toddy. The sun has set, so it is too late to spread the small millet in our leaf-screened courtyard for the doves and quails to eat. Therefore, even if it is only roasted hare, we will gladly share it with you. Enter and rest. The chief of this small village, where sparse-haired children harness the trembling-headed calf of a curved-horned wild cow as if it were a domestic calf, went yesterday on a mission sent by the king. When he returns, he will adorn your songstress with a garland and crown you with an unfading golden lotus.","modern":"செம்மண் பள்ளத்தில் தோண்டிய கிணற்றிலிருந்து எடுத்த கலங்கலான சிறிதளவு நீரைக் கொண்ட சிறிய வீட்டின் முற்றத்தில் இருந்த பழைய பானையில் இருந்த கள்ளை நாங்கள் குடித்துவிட்டதால் அது சுத்தமாக வறிதாகிவிட்டது. இலைகளால் வேய்ந்த முற்றத்தில் காயவைத்திருந்த சிறுதினையைப் புறாக்களும் காடைகளும் முழுமையாக உண்ணும்படி போடுவதற்குள் பொழுது போய்விட்டது. அதனால், முயல் கறியைச் சுட்டதாக இருந்தாலும் நாங்கள் உங்களுக்குத் தருவோம். அறிவுடைய பாணனே! உள்ளே வந்து இங்கே தங்குவாயாக. வளைந்த கொம்புகளையுடைய காட்டுப் பசுவின் நடுங்கும் தலையையுடைய கன்றினை, மெல்லிய தலையையுடைய சிறுவர்கள் வீட்டுக்கன்று போலப் பூட்டி விளையாடும் இந்தச் சிற்றூரின் தலைவன், நேற்று அரசன் ஏவிய தொழிலை மேற்கொண்டு சென்றிருக்கிறான். அவன் திரும்பி வந்து, உன்னுடைய பாடினிக்கு மாலையை அணிவித்து, உனக்கு வாடாத பொற்றாமரையைச் சூட்டுவான்.","explanation":"This poem belongs to the Vaakai tiṇai and Vallāṇ Mullai tuṟai, which praises the hospitality and valor of a warrior-chief of a small forest village. The speaker is the wife of the chief (or a villager) welcoming a wandering bard (pāṇaṉ) and his songstress (pāṭiṉi) at dusk. She explains that their jar of toddy is empty, and the sun has set, making it too late to dry millet for the birds, but they will gladly share roasted hare with the guests. She describes the rustic village where children playfully harness a wild cow's calf, showing the close connection to the forest landscape. She assures the bard that her husband, who is away on a royal mission, will return soon and reward them generously with garlands and golden lotuses. Key words include 'முதுவாய்ப் பாண' (wise bard), 'முயல்சுட்ட' (roasted hare), and 'வாடாத் தாமரை' (unfading lotus, symbolizing royal gold awards).","model":"gemini-3.5-flas